# Source: HoareLogic.pdf, section "Exercises", "A loop that counts down"; slides "ICT3112 - 6 - Loops".
id: countdown
title: A loop that counts down
topic: Loops
level: 1
source: HoareLogic.pdf, Exercises, "A loop that counts down"; Slides 6, Loops
tags: while, invariant, variant, termination

task:
  Find a loop invariant, and use the loop rule with consequence to prove
  that the triple is valid. Then give a variant for the loop, and show that
  the loop terminates.

pre: x = N && N >= 0
post: x = 0

program:
  {P}
  {I}
  while x > 0 do {
    {I && x > 0}
    {R1}
    x := x - 1
    {I}
  }
  {I && !(x > 0)}
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: Find a candidate invariant
rule: WHILE-CONSEQUENCE
focus: while
text:
  The invariant must hold every time the condition $x > 0$ is evaluated.
  From $x = N$ the loop takes the values $N$, $N - 1$, ..., $1$, $0$, and it
  stops at $0$. Every one of these values is at least $0$, including the
  last one, which is reached after the final iteration.
set: I = x >= 0
text:
  The rule WHILE-CONSEQUENCE needs three checks: establishment,
  preservation, and the postcondition at the exit.
instance: WHILE-CONSEQUENCE
  P ==> I
  {I && x > 0} x := x - 1 {I}
  I && !(x > 0) ==> Q
  ---
  {P} while x > 0 do x := x - 1 {Q}

step: Establishment
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
vc: x = N && N >= 0 ==> x >= 0
why:
  Replace $x$ by $N$: we need $N >= 0$, which is part of the precondition.

step: Preservation
rule: ASSIGN, CONSEQUENCE
focus: x := x - 1
calc:
  wp(x := x - 1, I)
  = (x >= 0)[x <- x - 1]
  = x - 1 >= 0
  = x >= 1
set: R1 = x >= 1
vc: x >= 0 && x > 0 ==> x >= 1
why:
  For integers, $x > 0$ is the same as $x >= 1$.

step: The postcondition at the exit
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
vc: x >= 0 && !(x > 0) ==> x = 0
why:
  $!(x > 0)$ is $x <= 0$. Together with $x >= 0$ it gives $x = 0$.

step: Termination
rule: WHILE-TOTAL
text:
  The loop decreases $x$ by one in every iteration, so we take the variant
  $E = x$. $E0$ is a logical variable for the value of $E$ at the start of
  an iteration.
vc: x >= 0 && x > 0 ==> x >= 0
why:
  The variant is not negative when an iteration starts.
vc: {x >= 0 && x > 0 && x = E0} x := x - 1 {x >= 0 && x < E0}
why:
  The weakest precondition of the postcondition is $x - 1 >= 0 && x - 1 < E0$.
  From $x > 0$ we get $x - 1 >= 0$, and from $x = E0$ we get
  $x - 1 = E0 - 1 < E0$.
result: The triple is valid, and the loop terminates.
